ta): {\"combined_score\": 1.0018272960697132, \"control_efficiency\": 0.9967096937452276, \"correct\": true, \"main_efficiency\": 1.0031066966508344, \"plan_validity_rate\": 1.0}\n- 2026-11-11T09:00:00Z | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: verified boundary polishing and transfer local search; gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=no new empirical evidence.\n  - evidence: idea_bundle.json + target_rubrics.json\n- unknown_ts | step=implement | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: added verified boundary polish (bisect shrink, drop redundant, pairwise transfer, fallback) to program_1/src/program.py; not evaluated\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-11T08:37:20.991926+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=1.003386466259221, correct=True; scientific acceptance passed; search metrics (data): {\"combined_score\": 1.003386466259221, \"control_efficiency\": 0.9967213029866018, \"correct\": true, \"main_efficiency\": 1.0050527570773755, \"plan_validity_rate\": 1.0}\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"selected_prompt_family\": \"paper_standalone\",\n  \"recommended_title\": \"Verified Minimal-Investment Planning for Leaderboard Rank Targeting under Multiple Aggregation Rules\",\n  \"central_question\": \"Under a declared per-task score-gain investment model, can a plan-verified planner find investments that make a target model strictly first on public multi-task leaderboards at a cost matching an evaluator-owned reference solver, across several aggregation rules, and how does it compare with naive investment baselines?\",\n  \"central_thesis\": \"On the sealed final evaluation over Open LLM Leaderboard snapshots, a verifier-checked investment planner (selected on HELM MMLU v1.13.0) reached a qualified outcome: its mean efficiency relative to the reference solver was 1.0032, versus 0.8166 for the better of two naive baselines, with every plan well-formed; the evidence supports a narrow, conditional claim about planner cost-efficiency under the stated investment model, not a general or causal claim about real developer training or about which aggregation rule is most robust.\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\": \"The grounded artifacts contain a completed, qualified final evaluation (final.qualification.outcome = qualified, status = completed, correct = true) with five metrics and two final seeds, plus a three-configuration search on HELM MMLU. They do NOT contain per-rule or per-target cost tables, so the original open question (how required investment varies with aggregation rule) cannot be answered with numbers from the artifacts. The strongest honest story is therefore a method-and-evaluation paper: formalize targeted investment (L1 point budget and L0 task count, caps, field, targets, success = strictly first), define the rule set, describe the verified planner and trusted verifier/reference/baselines protocol, and report efficiency relative to the reference with controls, while stating explicitly that rule-dependence of minimal cost is a hypothesis in the task contract and is not supported or refuted by retained metrics. Efficiency values slightly above 1 mean verified plans were on average marginally cheaper than the reference solver's (efficiency is capped at 2 per episode), so the reference is not claimed optimal and the planner is not claimed optimal either.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"rejected\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"Not in edit mode: no previous_paper directory or edit context exists. The framing is a fresh draft; the task brief's open-ended 'how does required investment vary by rule' question is retained as motivation, but the headline claim is narrowed to what the final evaluation measured.\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\": [\n    \"No staged prior manuscript or edit context was present; framing derived only from paper_data.json, task/task_contract.json, and the evaluator docstring.\",\n    \"paper_data.json has no downstream_study block; there is no frozen-selection vs baseline search-score comparison beyond the three search attempts, so no downstream-study claims are made.\"\n  ],\n  \"method_groups\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Verified minimal-investment planner (selected configuration: exact solvers for mean/median/maximin/plurality, verified heuristic or MILP level selection for trimmed/Borda/Copeland/z-score, plus verified boundary polishing and fallback)\",\n      \"role\": \"proposed\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"This is the studied method: the frozen search winner whose sealed final evaluation is the central result.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Naive investment baselines (uniform-gain and greedy marginal-cost; per case the better of the two)\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The only baseline with a retained final metric (baseline_main_efficiency = 0.8166); needed for the comparison that qualified the run.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Evaluator-owned reference solver\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Defines the efficiency scale (episode efficiency = min(2, reference cost / verified plan cost)); readers cannot interpret 1.003 without it.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Control conditions (permuted-input and within-task shuffled-score null)\",\n      \"role\": \"ablation\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"control_efficiency (final 1.0002; search 0.9967) is the retained diagnostic that the planner is not exploiting input order; report with the caveat that the retained value is an aggregate and that candidate diagnostics are marked unverified.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Search-stage configuration variants (basic solvers, +MILP level selection, +boundary polishing) on HELM MMLU v1.13.0\",\n      \"role\": \"ablation\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Three scored attempts (0 unscored) give a small incremental-component comparison: main efficiency 1.0031, 1.0031, 1.0051; the MILP layer yields identical combined score to the basic version, boundary polishing yields +0.0016 combined. Keep as an ablation, never as the main result, and keep separate from final results.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"results_focus\": [\n    \"Final (sealed, Open LLM Leaderboard snapshots, seeds 9001 and 9002): main efficiency 1.0032 vs naive-baseline 0.8166; control efficiency 1.0002; plan validity 1.0; qualified. Source ids: final.metric.main_efficiency, final.metric.baseline_main_efficiency, final.metric.control_efficiency, final.metric.plan_validity_rate, final.metric.combined_score, final.qualification.*, final.evidence.episodes.\",\n    \"Per-seed final episode scores (combined 1.00260 and 1.00257): the only variability evidence; report both values, state n=2 and that no confidence interval or significance test is available from the artifacts.\",\n    \"Search-stage component comparison on HELM MMLU (3 scored attempts, none unscored): main efficiency 1.0031 / 1.0031 / 1.0051, control 0.9967 / 0.9967 / 0.9967, validity 1.0; combined 1.00183 / 1.00183 / 1.00339. Report the +0.0016 gain from boundary polishing as small and unreplicated beyond 2 search seeds; the MILP layer showed no measured difference.\",\n    \"Search-to-final transfer: the selected configuration's main efficiency was 1.0051 on HELM search and 1.0032 on final boards; present as a descriptive observation, not as proof of generalization (different data, different boards).\",\n    \"Explicit scope statement: retained metrics contain no per-rule costs, so the claim that minimal required investment varies with aggregation rule is NOT evidenced here; list it as an untested hypothesis from the task contract and as the main limitation.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_plan\": [\n    \"Overview schematic (page 1-2): leaderboard score matrix -> investment model (per-task gains, L1 point budget with cap, L0 task count with cap at field-best) -> aggregation rules -> planner -> trusted verifier -> efficiency relative to reference; shows target strictly first as success.\",\n    \"Final-evaluation comparison: grouped bars for planner vs naive baselines vs reference line (efficiency=1) on the final boards, with per-seed markers.\",\n    \"Search-stage component comparison: grouped bars of main and control efficiency for the three configuration variants on HELM MMLU, with y-axis zoomed and labeled to show that differences are in the third decimal.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_specs\": [\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"overview_schematic\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Give immediate glance value for the problem: investment model, aggregation rules, planner/verifier pipeline, and how efficiency is defined relative to the reference solver.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"group_by\": \"pipeline_stage\",","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b5201c8-b8a7-4078-9f5d-fb9dc829679d","uuid":"4183a598-8379-4644-a924-47e0e985561c","timestamp":"2026-11-11T09:05:32.840Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"VENUE.md\nclaims_grounding.json\ndraft_reference_gaps_agent_claude_20261111_084048.log\ndraft_reference_gaps_prompt.md\nfigure_manifest.json\nfigures\nglobal_memory\nglobal_memory_recent.json\nglobal_memory_summary.md\nmemory.md\npaper.aux\npaper.bbl\npaper.blg\npaper.bst\npaper.log\npaper.out\npaper.pdf\npaper.sty\npaper.tex\npaper_agent_claude_20261111_084048.log\npaper_data.json\npaper_diagnostics\npaper_diagnostics_20261111_084048_71268.log\npaper_events_20261111_084048.jsonl\npaper_prep_20261111_084048.json\npaper_prompt.md\npaper_strategy.json\npaper_strategy.md\npaper_strategy_agent_claude_20261111_084048.log\npaper_strategy_prompt.md\npaper_strategy_resolution.json\nreference_query_plan.json\nreference_query_plan.md\nreference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261111_084048.log\nreference_query_plan_prompt.md\nreference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\nreference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\nreference_retrieval.log