{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgvsenz7TK37VTqYyAaR2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YXzQgqtSzor77d4mLrVzn6","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; cat > draft_reference_gaps.json <<'EOF'\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"summary\": \"The draft is mostly well-cited, but three paragraphs need a few targeted citations: cost-based bribery and rule definitions in computational social choice, the Open LLM Leaderboard as the held-out data source, and the introduction's incentive claim about rankings.\",\n  \"missing_literatures\": [\n    \"priced, swap and weighted bribery under Copeland, Borda and plurality\",\n    \"Open LLM Leaderboard (v2) documentation\",\n    \"Goodhart's law and metric gaming in ML evaluation\"\n  ],\n  \"rewrite_targets\": [\n    {\n      \"target_id\": \"related-work-social-choice-bribery\",\n      \"section\": \"related work\",\n      \"priority\": \"high\",\n      \"citation_intent\": \"comparator\",\n      \"reason\": \"The Computational social choice paragraph says its problem is a 'continuous variant' of manipulation, bribery and margin-of-victory problems. The cited works are generic or about election attacks. The paragraph cites no work on cost-priced or swap bribery, or on Copeland, Borda and plurality specifically, which are the rules the planner treats with greedy heuristics. Reviewers will expect these closest comparators.\",\n      \"anchor_text\": \"Our problem is a continuous variant: the ``voters'' are tasks, the intervention changes one candidate's scores\",\n      \"search_topics\": [\n        \"priced bribery and swap bribery in elections\",\n        \"Copeland, Borda and plurality bribery and control complexity\",\n        \"bribery or manipulation under score-based or range voting with cost per voter\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"target_id\": \"heldout-data-open-llm-leaderboard\",\n      \"section\": \"related work\",